# LLVM version migration (9 → 22)

Target is **LLVM 22.1.x**, installable on jammy from `apt.llvm.org/jammy llvm-toolchain-jammy-22`.
Full plan: `/root/plans/php-compiler-release/LLVM22-MIGRATION.md`.

## The one fact that shapes everything

**Typed-pointer builders no longer exist.** Probed against a real LLVM 22.1.8 install:

```
LLVMBuildLoad / LLVMBuildCall / LLVMBuildGEP in Core.h  →  0 occurrences
LLVMBuildLoad2 / Call2 / GEP2                           →  the only option
llvm-c/Transforms/                                      →  only PassBuilder.h
libLLVM-22.so                                           →  no ".1" suffix
```

They are **not** present as deprecated shims, so there is no "get it loading first, migrate
gradually" path — nothing compiles until every `load`/`gep`/`call` carries an explicit type, and
`LLVMGetElementType()` on a pointer is gone.

## Where the work belongs

~4,100 call sites in `lib/` need a type (`load` 1,026, `structGep` 797, `call` 2,226, `gep` 65) —
but they all funnel through `LLVMAbstract\Builder`. **Do not edit call sites.** Track the pointee
type on the PHP `Value` wrapper at every point a pointer is produced (alloca, GEP, global,
bitcast/pointerCast, call return, parameter) and let the binding feed the `*2` builders.

## Do the threading on LLVM 9 first

Under LLVM 9 the pointee type is still derivable, so threading it is a **no-op that can be validated
against the working compiler**. Any behaviour difference is a bug in the threading, caught while the
old backend still works.

Only switch backends once the sweep and compliance comparison are clean. This splits one large risky
change into two verifiable ones — which matters because there is **no CI on `lib/`** (see
`correctness-gates.mdc`).

## Additive, therefore reversible

`Chooser` already dispatches across `LLVM4/7/8/9`. Add `LLVM22` alongside rather than replacing —
both stay selectable during transition, and that is the rollback. `Chooser::FILES` needs a new entry
for `libLLVM-22.so`; the existing map only matches `.so.1` names and would silently never select it.

## Regenerate every LLVM-version-specific artifact

Objects built by LLVM 9 cannot be linked with LLVM 22 output and trusted:

* `prelinked/helper-runtime/x86_64-linux/**` — 257 objects emitted by LLVM 9
* `prelinked/bootstrap-gen0/**`, `prelinked/bootstrap-vendor/**`
* pinned image `Docker/dev/ubuntu-22.04/Dockerfile`, `script/install-llvm9.sh`
* link flags / sysroot in `lib/AOT/Linker.php`

## Traps already hit

* **IR text is not portable across versions.** LLVM 9 emits `void*`; clang 14+ rejects it with
  `pointers to void are invalid - use i8*`. Expect more of this class when comparing or reusing IR.
* `PassManagerBuilder` is gone — the replacement `LLVMRunPasses(M, "default<O2>", TM, opts)` is
  *simpler* than the legacy plumbing added in #23503. Version-gate rather than rewrite in place.
* MCJIT is legacy; `createExecutionEngine`/`getTargetMachine` (the object-emit path) may need
  reworking for ORC.
* The binding generator is `ircmaxell/ffime:dev-master` and needs `minimum-stability: dev` — a plain
  `composer require ircmaxell/ffime` fails with a usage dump rather than a clear error.

## Keep it separate from performance work

A newer optimiser does **not** fix interpreter-shaped IR — enabling the O2 pipeline bought only 16%
because boxed values and runtime dispatch defeat it. This migration is modernisation; the speculation
experiments (#23483) are the performance lever. Do not entangle them.
